AT in semantic parsing, IE, structured prediction (no specific citations)\n\nTotal references: ~7 cited papers.\n\n## Key Claims\n1. SICA provides no significant accuracy gain over SC across 5 models, 6 domains, N>1300\n2. Mechanistic explanation: MAX-SAT ≈ counting ≈ random-weight (99.4% agreement) because constraints lack discriminative power\n3. C0 &lt; 5% as zero-cost binary early-stopping gate\n\n## Results Summary\n- Table 1: 9 conditions, Δ from -5.9 to +5.0pp, no p &lt; 0.10\n- Table 2: constraint ablation, 99.4% agreement across methods\n- Additional: temperature ablation, K ablation, weighted SC comparison, tiebreak analysis, Logic-LM comparison, overhead analysis, scaling analysis\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty of contribution**: Is the \"capability trap\" concept novel? Is the negative finding sufficiently surprising to warrant publication? Does this paper tell the community something it doesn't already know?\n2. **Positioning relative to prior work**: Is the related work comprehensive? Are key works missing? Is the paper's contribution clearly differentiated from prior work?\n3. **Conceptual contribution**: Is the \"capability trap\" framing insightful or merely descriptive? Does C0 as a diagnostic add value?\n4. **Scope of claims vs evidence**: Are the claims appropriately scoped given the evidence? Is one symbolic method (SICA) sufficient to conclude about \"symbolic consensus\" generally?\n5. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-written? Does it follow academic conventions?\n6. **Citation coverage**: Are there important missing citations? Is the related work a survey or does it position the paper?\n\nConsider these potential concerns:\n- The paper tests ONE symbolic method (SICA) but titles/claims refer to \"symbolic consensus\" generally\n- Only ~7 references — is this sufficient for a top venue?\n- The negative result may be unsurprising to practitioners who have tried neuro-symbolic methods\n- \"Capability trap\" may be a redescription rather than a new insight\n- No comparison with trained verifiers (ORM/PRM) — only mentioned in related work\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted)\n- Questions for authors\n- Missing related work or positioning issues\n- Score (1-6) with confidence (low/medium/high)\n- One-line recommendation\n\nBe rigorous. Negative-result papers must offer genuine insight, not just \"we tried X and it didn't work.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014ywVxmwKdSp41GtgAJEWPH","session_id":"1bafea0c-3dcc-4cf8-a7a2-52faa8778916","uuid":"7060baec-8ec9-4630-b5c7-aa318fa55eae","timestamp":"2026-05-17T15:54:24.872Z"}
